---
name: NXLog Windows Event JSON → OCSF
description: |
  Read Windows Event Log records that NXLog's `im_msvistalog` module collects
  and `to_json()` writes, and normalize them to OCSF.

  With NXLog Enterprise Edition, configure `CaptureEventXML TRUE`. NXLog then
  adds the event XML, which yields the same OCSF events as native collection.

  NXLog Community Edition supports neither `CaptureEventXML` nor `AddPrefix`.
  It drops unnamed `<Data>` elements, `<Binary>`, and `UserData`, so their
  values are lost. It also overwrites event data fields that share a name with
  NXLog fields, such as `ProcessId`, `EventType`, or `AccountName`. The
  normalizer recovers these values from the English message of Sysmon,
  Security-Auditing, Application Error 1000, and Service Control Manager 7045
  events, but not on Windows in other languages. NXLog CE writes event times
  in the local time of the host without a time zone, which read as UTC.
  Configure it to write exact times as microseconds since the Unix epoch:

  ```
  <Input eventlog>
    Module im_msvistalog
    Exec $EventTime = integer($EventTime); $EventReceivedTime = integer($EventReceivedTime);
  </Input>

  <Output file>
    Module om_file
    File 'C:\ProgramData\nxlog\eventlog.ndjson'
    Exec to_json();
  </Output>
  ```
---

from_file "nxlog.ndjson" {
  read_lines
}
// `raw=true` keeps event data values as NXLog writes them, instead of
// guessing types, such as a time for a Windows timestamp.
event = line.parse_json(raw=true, unflatten_separator=".")
microsoft::windows::ocsf::normalize event, into=ocsf
ocsf.metadata.log_format = "json"
ocsf.raw_data = move line
ocsf.raw_data_size = ocsf.raw_data.length_bytes()
this = ocsf_cast(ocsf_derive(ocsf))
